  "prompt_text": "This figure presents a broad urban design concept showing how green infrastructure can deliver multiple benefits across a city streetscape and stream corridor. Trees, rain gardens, swales, infiltration trenches, and a restored urban stream are arranged from roadside to open space, illustrating how stormwater is captured, filtered, and infiltrated into the ground rather than simply running off paved surfaces. Blue arrows below the surface emphasize water moving downward into soil.\n\nThe image links these landscape elements to several urban co-benefits. Vegetation is shown improving property values, reducing air pollution and taking up CO2, and providing protection from wind and noise. Street and park trees also create shade and cooling, while their canopies intercept and evaporate rainfall to reduce runoff. Open green areas support both passive and active recreation, with a cyclist and runner suggesting everyday public use.\n\nToward the right, a “healthy urban stream corridor” appears to filter flow, provide habitat, and safely convey floodwaters. Across the whole scene, the message is that connected green spaces and water-sensitive design can simultaneously manage stormwater, improve urban climate and amenity, and enhance biodiversity within a dense city environment.",
